{
  "status": "complete",
  "summary": "Research across Anthropic, CrewAI, AG2, LangChain, OpenAI Agents/Swarm, Ollama, OpenCode, and A2A shows a clean split between isolated-worker patterns and handoff/team patterns. Your current review flow matches the isolated-worker family: a local supervisor delegates bounded tasks, workers return structured findings, and synthesis stays local. The smallest portable abstraction is therefore a repo-owned worker execution adapter with static worker profiles, dynamic task invocations, and local schema validation after every run. Official docs are useful but vendor-biased: each runtime presents its own primitives as the default, and none of the named runtimes exposes a shared backend-neutral worker-result contract.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "claim": "The repo already points toward a backend-agnostic worker contract, but the execution step is still hardcoded to Codex CLI.",
      "source": "C:/Users/minhl/OneDrive/Desktop/llmsandbox/skills/review/SKILL.md | C:/Users/minhl/OneDrive/Desktop/llmsandbox/codex-prompts/review-skill/review-skill-2026-03-25.md",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "`skills/review/SKILL.md` launches `codex exec --full-auto --json`; the local design note calls for an 'input prompt + output schema' abstraction."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Anthropic's subagent model is a strong conceptual match for review workers because it uses isolated contexts, configurable tools/permissions, and optional concurrent background execution.",
      "source": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Docs say each subagent has 'its own context window'; background subagents 'run concurrently'."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Anthropic agent teams are a different, heavier backend pattern intended for peer coordination rather than simple worker fan-out.",
      "source": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-teams",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "A 'team lead' coordinates teammates that 'communicate directly', and the comparison table marks token cost as 'Higher'."
    },
    {
      "claim": "CrewAI's task interface is a useful template for a portable worker contract because it separates task invocation from agent identity and includes native schema and validation hooks.",
      "source": "https://docs.crewai.com/en/concepts/tasks",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Task fields include `expected_output`, `agent`, `context`, `output_json`, `output_pydantic`, `guardrail`, and `guardrail_max_retries`."
    },
    {
      "claim": "AG2 defines delegation primarily as transition logic, where tools can return routing targets plus updated context, which is richer than a one-shot review worker needs.",
      "source": "https://docs.ag2.ai/latest/docs/user-guide/advanced-concepts/orchestration/group-chat/handoffs/",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "evidence": "Docs show `ReplyResult(message, target, context_variables)` and say AG2 has 'four main ways' to define handoffs."
    },
    {
      "claim": "LangChain's subagent pattern is closer to this review workflow than its handoff pattern: subagents are stateless and isolated, while handoffs are state-driven conversational transfers.",
      "source": "https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/multi-agent/subagents | https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/multi-agent/handoffs | https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/multi-agent/index",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Subagents are 'stateless' with a 'clean context window'; the overview says they add 'one extra call' in exchange for 'centralized control'."
    },
    {
      "claim": "OpenAI's Agents SDK explicitly separates handoffs from nested worker execution, which supports using a worker-as-tool adapter instead of making handoffs the universal abstraction.",
      "source": "https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/handoffs/",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Docs expose `input_type`, `on_handoff`, and `input_filter`, and recommend `Agent.as_tool(parameters=...)` for a 'nested specialist'."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Swarm is a good conceptual minimum for agent orchestration, but it remains handoff-centric and does not provide a portable worker-result contract by itself.",
      "source": "https://github.com/openai/swarm",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Swarm exposes two primitives, `Agent`s and handoffs, and is 'stateless between calls'."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Alternative execution backends are already viable: Claude Code CLI exposes machine-readable JSON transport, Ollama exposes JSON-schema-constrained output, and OpenCode exposes provider/model and subagent permission controls.",
      "source": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/common-workflows | https://docs.ollama.com/capabilities/structured-outputs | https://opencode.ai/docs/agents/",
      "confidence": "high",
      "evidence": "Anthropic examples use `--output-format json`; Ollama says use `format` with JSON schema; OpenCode documents `provider/model-id` and `permission.task`."
    },
    {
      "claim": "A2A is the clearest current protocol-level option for cross-agent interoperability, but it solves a broader service-to-service problem than the minimal backend adapter needed here.",
      "source": "https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/topics/what-is-a2a/ | https://a2a-protocol.org/latest/specification/",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "evidence": "A2A standardizes agent communication, supports long-running tasks, and defines Agent Cards, tasks, messages, artifacts, and streaming."
    }
  ],
  "artifacts": [
    {
      "type": "file_created",
      "path": "C:/Users/minhl/OneDrive/Desktop/llmsandbox/discoveries/multi-agent-backend-abstraction-2026-03-25.md",
      "description": "Full discovery report with comparison matrix, recommendation, quick wins, and source list.",
      "content": "Saved research report covering repo context, framework comparisons, recommended adapter layers, and work to avoid."
    },
    {
      "type": "code_sample",
      "path": "worker-backend-interface",
      "description": "Minimal portable worker abstraction that preserves the current supervisor-plus-worker review flow while allowing backend swaps.",
      "content": "type WorkerProfile = {\n  id: string\n  description: string\n  systemPrompt?: string\n  model?: string\n  tools?: string[]\n  permissions?: Record<string, string>\n  backendHints?: {\n    runtime?: string\n    effort?: 'low' | 'medium' | 'high'\n    background?: boolean\n  }\n}\n\ntype WorkerRequest = {\n  workerProfileId: string\n  prompt: string\n  outputSchema: object\n  contextFiles?: string[]\n  attachments?: string[]\n  timeoutMs?: number\n  metadata?: Record<string, unknown>\n}\n\ntype WorkerResult<T = unknown> = {\n  status: 'complete' | 'partial' | 'failed'\n  structured: T | null\n  rawText: string\n  events?: unknown[]\n  usage?: {\n    inputTokens?: number\n    outputTokens?: number\n    durationMs?: number\n    costUsd?: number\n  }\n  error?: {\n    code: string\n    message: string\n    retryable: boolean\n  }\n}\n\ninterface WorkerBackend {\n  name: string\n  capabilities(): {\n    nativeSchema: boolean\n    streaming: boolean\n    background: boolean\n    isolatedContext: boolean\n    toolsConfigurable: boolean\n    permissionsConfigurable: boolean\n  }\n  execute<T>(request: WorkerRequest): Promise<WorkerResult<T>>\n}"
    }
  ],
  "gaps": [
    "I did not find an official shared worker-result schema used across Anthropic, OpenAI Agents, LangChain, CrewAI, and AG2; each runtime defines its own native primitives.",
    "Several official docs are living documentation without explicit publication dates, so recency had to be inferred from current version markers like CrewAI 1.11.1, AG2 0.11.4, and Agents SDK 0.13.1.",
    "Claude Code and OpenCode docs clearly document agent configuration and JSON transport, but they do not document a backend-neutral typed worker output contract comparable to your current Codex schema."
  ],
  "escalations": [
    "Decide whether the first abstraction target is only isolated workers or also handoff/team semantics; those are materially different APIs and should not be conflated.",
    "Make local schema validation mandatory even when a backend advertises native schema support; this is the main trust boundary that preserves portability.",
    "If remote worker services or cross-organization agent interoperability are near-term goals, scope A2A as a second protocol layer instead of folding that complexity into the first adapter."
  ]
}
